validate_subscriptions_on_store.s:
Fourth root cause: ws/src/hub.ts /sub (lines 187-199) stores ANY body with no format check — `const body = (await request.json()) as { ep: string; k: string; au: string; ua?: string };` then puts a SubEntry. Garbage p256dh/auth/endpoint (from an interrupted enable, a re-installed PWA, a bot, or a buggy client) is what seeds the corruption that isolate_push_fanout_failures then has to clean up. Since EVERY storage path funnels through this one DO route (src/routes/api/push/+server.ts POST -> subs.ts save_sub -> hub_client.ts hub_sub -> ws/src/index.ts /hub/:uid/sub -> this handler), validating here is the single choke point. Reject invalid subscriptions with a 400 so garbage never enters storage.

Add to src/lib/server/push.ts (it already exports unb64u import, and hub.ts already imports from it):
```ts
export function valid_sub(ep: string, k: string, au: string): boolean {
	try {
		let url: URL;
		try {
			url = new URL(ep);
		} catch {
			return false;
		}
		if (url.protocol !== 'https:') return false;
		const pub = unb64u(k); // atob throws on non-base64 — caught below
		if (pub.length !== 65 || pub[0] !== 0x04) return false;
		return unb64u(au).length === 16;
	} catch {
		return false;
	}
}
```

In ws/src/hub.ts, extend the push import (line 2) to include valid_sub, and change /sub (current lines 187-199):
```ts
		if (url.pathname === '/sub' && request.method === 'POST') {
			const body = (await request.json()) as { ep: string; k: string; au: string; ua?: string };
			const key = 'sub:' + (await uuid_from(body.ep));
			const entry: SubEntry = {
				ep: body.ep,
				k: body.k,
				au: body.au,
				...(body.ua ? { ua: body.ua } : {}),
				d: Date.now()
			};
			await this.state.storage.put(key, entry);
			return new Response('ok');
		}
```
becomes:
```ts
		if (url.pathname === '/sub' && request.method === 'POST') {
			const body = (await request.json()) as { ep: string; k: string; au: string; ua?: string };
			if (!valid_sub(body.ep, body.k, body.au))
				return new Response('invalid subscription', { status: 400 });
			const key = 'sub:' + (await uuid_from(body.ep));
			const entry: SubEntry = {
				ep: body.ep,
				k: body.k,
				au: body.au,
				...(body.ua ? { ua: body.ua } : {}),
				d: Date.now()
			};
			await this.state.storage.put(key, entry);
			return new Response('ok');
		}
```

Why these exact rules: Web Push p256dh is always the 65-byte uncompressed P-256 point (0x04 prefix), auth is always 16 bytes, endpoints are always https. This matches what valid_sub in harden_send_push_bad_sub's catch already treats as gone, so validation and pruning agree.

